## Context

Author directive, 2026-10-26: print the duration of every verify-gate, gaze,
review and merge.

Incident (this session, 2026-10-26): landing PR 1667, a three-line addition and
fifteen-line deletion of one paragraph of `skills/gaze/SKILL.md` that the
author had already agreed to, took about ten minutes. Nobody can say where the
time went: gaze writes `wall=na tokens=na` into its telemetry line, the gate
prints no duration, and a merge prints none either. Only the harness's own task
notifications carry a figure (the 1667 gaze fork: 153 s), and only after the
fact. The cost of the review path is therefore argued, not measured.

Related: [[1197]] (measure where gaze spends its wall time), [[1184]] (measure
one raid by phase against the 20-minute review budget), [[1221]] (merge gating
proportional to the diff).

## Exit criteria

- [ ] `/gaze`, `/verify-gate` and `/merge` each end by printing their own wall-clock duration on one stable line (for example `duration: 4m12s`), in the returned result and in the comment they post on the merge request; `wall=na` is replaced by the measured value.
- [ ] A duration comes from a clock read at the start and at the end of the step, never from the model's estimate; a step that cannot read a clock prints `duration: not measured`, not a guess.
- [ ] Each review seat inside `/gaze` (and each seat of `/verify-gate`, if any) prints its own duration, so the slowest seat is visible without reading transcripts.
- [ ] `/raid`'s wrap-up briefing prints one table per merge request: gaze, each gate round, merge, and the total from first gaze to MERGED, and names the slowest step.
- [ ] A regression test runs a stubbed step and asserts the duration line is present, well formed and measured (a stub that sleeps a known interval reads back at least that interval).
- [ ] Ticket 1184 (measure one raid by phase) is closed when the raid table above has been printed for one real raid; this ticket subsumes its measurement.
- [ ] The harness gate (`.merge-gate.json` checks) passes.

